WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:02.501
-My first guest is one of

00:00:02.501 --> 00:00:04.437
the most talented people
on the planet.

00:00:04.437 --> 00:00:05.805
He's a 10-time Grammy winner,

00:00:05.805 --> 00:00:09.475
a four-time Emmy winner,
and an Oscar-nominated artist.

00:00:09.475 --> 00:00:11.411
He stars
in the new film "Palmer,"

00:00:11.411 --> 00:00:13.879
which is streaming now
on Apple TV+.

00:00:13.879 --> 00:00:16.282
Here is Justin Timberlake.

00:00:16.282 --> 00:00:19.485
-What do you want?
-[ Laughs ]

00:00:19.485 --> 00:00:20.787
-What do you want?

00:00:20.787 --> 00:00:22.989
-What do you want? Hey!

00:00:22.989 --> 00:00:25.124
-Hey! What do you want?
-Hey, what do you want?

00:00:25.124 --> 00:00:27.093
Ah! Look, that's what
I'm talking about.

00:00:27.093 --> 00:00:29.895
Look at that.
That's my man right there.

00:00:29.895 --> 00:00:31.864
Fresh cut.

00:00:31.864 --> 00:00:35.468
-You look like you are -- You
just went on vacation.

00:00:35.468 --> 00:00:37.037
I look --
-I know.

00:00:37.037 --> 00:00:39.172
I weirdly -- I look tan.
I think it's --

00:00:39.172 --> 00:00:40.874
-Why is that light so bright?

00:00:40.874 --> 00:00:42.809
-I think it's
high blood pressure, too.

00:00:42.809 --> 00:00:44.710
-Oh, right.
Well, every time you're --

00:00:44.710 --> 00:00:45.845
-Also -- Yeah.

00:00:45.845 --> 00:00:48.615
And I wear
tight collared shirts.

00:00:48.615 --> 00:00:51.150
-♪ He wears
tight collared shirts ♪

00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:54.320
-♪ He wears tight
collared shirts ♪

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:55.721
♪ That's why we all know him ♪

00:00:55.721 --> 00:00:58.691
♪ He wears
tight collared shirts ♪

00:00:58.691 --> 00:01:00.459
♪ That's why we'll show him ♪

00:01:00.459 --> 00:01:01.728
[ Clears throat ]

00:01:01.728 --> 00:01:04.563
-♪ There's a birthmark he's
insecure about ♪

00:01:04.563 --> 00:01:06.833
♪ A birthmark he's insecure ♪

00:01:06.833 --> 00:01:09.536
-♪ That dude wears
tight collared shirts ♪

00:01:09.536 --> 00:01:13.139
♪ I could pick him out
in a crowd ♪

00:01:13.139 --> 00:01:16.408
Dude, I have to just --
How's Jessica doing?

00:01:16.408 --> 00:01:20.546
I know she's doing well,
but how is the baby?

00:01:20.546 --> 00:01:23.349
-You mean me? I'm doing great.
-No. Come on.

00:01:23.349 --> 00:01:26.185
The little --
Dude, he's so cute.

00:01:26.185 --> 00:01:30.556
-No, but -- I know, man.
Nobody's sleeping.

00:01:30.556 --> 00:01:33.059
But he's so cute.

00:01:33.059 --> 00:01:35.928
-Oh, dude, I remember
when you guys FaceTimed us.

00:01:35.928 --> 00:01:37.163
We didn't know anything.

00:01:37.163 --> 00:01:38.230
You know, we knew it was
happening soon,

00:01:38.230 --> 00:01:39.432
but we didn't know when.

00:01:39.432 --> 00:01:43.002
And, dude, we were just like,
"What's up, man?"

00:01:43.002 --> 00:01:44.670
Just waterworks, dude.
-That's right.

00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:47.506
-I was like, "Oh, my gosh."
And then he did a bit.

00:01:47.506 --> 00:01:49.208
Do you remember?
He did his first bit.

00:01:49.208 --> 00:01:50.442
-He did.

00:01:50.442 --> 00:01:53.412
-He put his face up
against that little, like,

00:01:53.412 --> 00:01:55.848
plastic thing,
and he went like...

00:01:55.848 --> 00:01:57.150
and, like, blew.

00:01:57.150 --> 00:01:59.585
The old classic bit.
He'll do that forever.

00:01:59.585 --> 00:02:01.821
-I wish I would have had
the ability to record that

00:02:01.821 --> 00:02:03.455
so I could see it later.

00:02:03.455 --> 00:02:06.359
-It was perfect timing, man.
It was so funny.

00:02:06.359 --> 00:02:07.460
-Yeah.

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:09.929
-Hey, you have a big weekend
coming up.

00:02:09.929 --> 00:02:11.964
This Sunday is
your 40th birthday.

00:02:11.964 --> 00:02:13.633
Happy birthday!

00:02:13.633 --> 00:02:15.868
-Thanks for remembering, bro.
-Oh, no problem.

00:02:15.868 --> 00:02:18.304
Dude, my segment producer
told me to bring it up.

00:02:18.304 --> 00:02:22.941
-Yeah, yeah. Yeah, I figured.
-How does it feel?

00:02:22.941 --> 00:02:27.213
I can't --
You don't feel 40 to me.

00:02:27.213 --> 00:02:30.183
-I mean, you don't feel
50 to me, and so --

00:02:30.183 --> 00:02:33.786
-I'm not 50.
-Oh! Oh.

00:02:33.786 --> 00:02:37.757
-I'm 41, dude.
We're so close in age.

00:02:37.757 --> 00:02:40.326
See? You always forget that.
-Right.

00:02:40.326 --> 00:02:43.128
-I got to say,
you've been very busy lately.

00:02:43.128 --> 00:02:45.798
You were active
in getting people out to vote,

00:02:45.798 --> 00:02:47.333
which worked, by the way,

00:02:47.333 --> 00:02:50.636
both for the presidential
election and the Georgia runoff.

00:02:50.636 --> 00:02:54.207
But, man, you crushed it
at the inauguration.

00:02:54.207 --> 00:02:56.642
How did you -- How did that
performance come together?

00:02:56.642 --> 00:02:58.143
Who talked to who?

00:02:58.143 --> 00:03:01.947
-Well, you know, it was --

00:03:01.947 --> 00:03:04.917
I had written a song
with Ant Clemons,

00:03:04.917 --> 00:03:06.519
an up-and-coming artist,

00:03:06.519 --> 00:03:11.191
and we've written a song,
"Better Days."

00:03:11.191 --> 00:03:14.059
And we were doing
these writing sessions

00:03:14.059 --> 00:03:16.629
and we just came --
We started writing this song,

00:03:16.629 --> 00:03:17.997
and, ironically,

00:03:17.997 --> 00:03:24.303
I recorded my final vocal
for the song on Election Day

00:03:24.303 --> 00:03:27.507
just because I was so nervous
about what was going to happen.

00:03:27.507 --> 00:03:30.109
I was like --
I had to distract myself.

00:03:30.109 --> 00:03:34.847
And I don't know if, like,

00:03:34.847 --> 00:03:40.352
somebody who was on my side
communicated to them or they --

00:03:40.352 --> 00:03:41.821
I'm just gonna go with I think,

00:03:41.821 --> 00:03:43.823
you know, I'm pretty sure
Joe and Kamala

00:03:43.823 --> 00:03:46.893
just wanted us to do it.

00:03:46.893 --> 00:03:49.828
And, no, we just heard
there was an opportunity

00:03:49.828 --> 00:03:51.430
to perform at the inauguration,

00:03:51.430 --> 00:03:54.733
and we were
super-stoked to do it.

00:03:54.733 --> 00:03:57.470
-You don't mess around.
And It's not the first time.

00:03:57.470 --> 00:04:00.639
You actually performed
for President Obama.

00:04:00.639 --> 00:04:05.010
What were you doing?
What jam were you doing there?

00:04:05.010 --> 00:04:07.680
-Obama looks like he wants
to get in on that.

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:09.182
-Yeah.
-Go back to that picture.

00:04:09.182 --> 00:04:11.951
He looks like --
He's like, "I'm just waiting.

00:04:11.951 --> 00:04:13.252
I'm waiting for the invitation."

00:04:13.252 --> 00:04:15.054
-No, he's a performer.
Yeah, he does. He does.

00:04:15.054 --> 00:04:16.155
Exactly right. Yeah.

00:04:16.155 --> 00:04:21.094
-So, they used to --
I think the Obamas

00:04:21.094 --> 00:04:26.799
did a certain theme every summer
at The White House with music,

00:04:26.799 --> 00:04:29.068
and that year, it was Memphis.

00:04:29.068 --> 00:04:31.637
It was Memphis and Stax.

00:04:31.637 --> 00:04:37.010
And so they had Booker from
Booker T. and the M.G.'s

00:04:37.010 --> 00:04:39.111
and they had Steve Cropper,

00:04:39.111 --> 00:04:42.548
who was the guitarist
on so many of those records.

00:04:42.548 --> 00:04:44.517
And Steve Cropper plays

00:04:44.517 --> 00:04:48.253
on the famous "(Sittin' On)
The Dock of the Bay."

00:04:48.253 --> 00:04:53.859
And so I got to come and perform
that one at The White House.

00:04:53.859 --> 00:04:57.096
It was
pretty, pretty, pretty good.

00:04:57.096 --> 00:05:00.132
-Yeah. Do you get nervous?

00:05:00.132 --> 00:05:02.135
-I was nervous for that one.
-Yeah.

00:05:02.135 --> 00:05:03.469
-I was nervous for that one

00:05:03.469 --> 00:05:06.005
because the president
and the First Lady

00:05:06.005 --> 00:05:08.574
were both sitting right there.

00:05:08.574 --> 00:05:12.945
My wife and my parents
were not far away.

00:05:12.945 --> 00:05:14.046
-That's a cool moment.

00:05:14.046 --> 00:05:15.548
-But, yeah, it's --
I don't know.

00:05:15.548 --> 00:05:18.084
It was just -- It felt like --

00:05:18.084 --> 00:05:22.155
And, also, like,
that's a classic,

00:05:22.155 --> 00:05:24.257
you know, so you're like,
"Man, don't botch this."

00:05:24.257 --> 00:05:27.092
-Don't mess this one up, yeah.
-Don't botch this.

00:05:27.092 --> 00:05:28.494
-I was keeping up
with you, obviously,

00:05:28.494 --> 00:05:31.431
during the whole quarantine,

00:05:31.431 --> 00:05:32.965
and I just -- I knew,
in your head,

00:05:32.965 --> 00:05:34.199
you can't stop your brain.

00:05:34.199 --> 00:05:36.469
So I was like,
"Are you working on something?"

00:05:36.469 --> 00:05:38.404
You know, but I always hate
to just bother you.

00:05:38.404 --> 00:05:40.339
And then you posted
some Instagram photo of you

00:05:40.339 --> 00:05:43.576
and Timbaland
back together working on stuff.

00:05:43.576 --> 00:05:45.278
Can we say?

00:05:45.278 --> 00:05:47.146
Is there a possibility
that there's going to be

00:05:47.146 --> 00:05:49.682
a new Justin Timberlake album
in the works?

00:05:52.184 --> 00:05:55.655
-Yeah, we could say that.

00:05:55.655 --> 00:05:58.523
-Alright. Yeah, we just did.
We said that.

00:05:58.523 --> 00:06:02.261
-There's a possibility.
I mean, let's go with "yes."

00:06:02.261 --> 00:06:06.732
I mean, I've been in and out
of the studio working on stuff,

00:06:06.732 --> 00:06:09.602
and I played you a few tunes.

00:06:09.602 --> 00:06:12.638
Let's just tell them.
I played you a few tunes.

00:06:12.638 --> 00:06:15.441
-Let's just say...

00:06:15.441 --> 00:06:19.445
Dude, I cannot -- I cannot wait.

00:06:19.445 --> 00:06:21.947
How do you have the patience
to just not just go,

00:06:21.947 --> 00:06:23.115
"Yeah, just listen to all this.

00:06:23.115 --> 00:06:24.416
Just get it out."

00:06:24.416 --> 00:06:28.421
Like, how -- It's just like
you've got to be Zen.

00:06:28.421 --> 00:06:30.422
Because they're good songs.

00:06:30.422 --> 00:06:34.093
-Well, thanks, man.
I don't know.

00:06:34.093 --> 00:06:35.794
I mean, listen,
I sat on "Mirrors"

00:06:35.794 --> 00:06:40.199
for like four or five years
and almost got to the point

00:06:40.199 --> 00:06:42.301
that I was like,
"What was that song we did?"

00:06:42.301 --> 00:06:44.303
And --
-Really?

00:06:44.303 --> 00:06:45.437
-Yeah. I don't know.

00:06:45.437 --> 00:06:48.540
I think I --
I think I just take my time.

00:06:48.540 --> 00:06:49.842
Maybe that's my barometer,

00:06:49.842 --> 00:06:53.011
that if I still love them
as much after time goes by,

00:06:53.011 --> 00:06:55.514
then hopefully people will

00:06:55.514 --> 00:06:57.883
after they hear them
when time goes by.

00:06:57.883 --> 00:07:01.186
-You've collaborated with,
I think, everyone at this point.

00:07:01.186 --> 00:07:03.890
Is there anyone
that you haven't worked with yet

00:07:03.890 --> 00:07:05.524
that you'd love
to get in the studio with?

00:07:05.524 --> 00:07:10.796
-Kendrick is on top of my list.
Kendrick, Travis Scott.

00:07:10.796 --> 00:07:14.567
-Yeah, that's fun.
That would be a good mix.

00:07:14.567 --> 00:07:15.768
That would fan-- You know --
-I don't know.

00:07:15.768 --> 00:07:17.703
Who else? Who else?

00:07:17.703 --> 00:07:21.006
-Well, I mean, there's
certain -- Some talk-show host

00:07:21.006 --> 00:07:25.177
can sing and, you know...

00:07:25.177 --> 00:07:26.979
-Oh, really?

00:07:26.979 --> 00:07:29.781
-Yeah. I mean --
-Does, uh...

00:07:29.781 --> 00:07:35.354
Does Colbert sing?
-[ Laughs ]

00:07:35.354 --> 00:07:36.688
Hey, Paul McCartney
was on our show recently,

00:07:36.688 --> 00:07:37.722
and we talked about --

00:07:37.722 --> 00:07:38.857
-No, hold on, hold on.

00:07:38.857 --> 00:07:40.893
Listen, I'll happily put you
on a record

00:07:40.893 --> 00:07:42.428
if you want to be on a record.

00:07:42.428 --> 00:07:43.896
You want to
sing some backgrounds?

00:07:43.896 --> 00:07:45.598
-Yeah.

00:07:45.598 --> 00:07:48.333
-Okay. Done.
-This just happened?

00:07:48.333 --> 00:07:49.534
We can't edit this out.
-Yeah.

00:07:49.534 --> 00:07:50.869
-We have
to leave this in the show.

00:07:50.869 --> 00:07:53.573
-But I'm going to treat you
the same way that I did

00:07:53.573 --> 00:07:58.110
right before we did the first
Bee Gees sketch we ever did.

00:07:58.110 --> 00:08:00.112
-Forget about it. I'm out.

00:08:00.112 --> 00:08:02.381
Dude, you're too much of
a perfectionist.

00:08:02.381 --> 00:08:03.482
Like, I don't know if
I could do it.

00:08:03.482 --> 00:08:05.451
You're like,
"Dude, you're flat again."

00:08:05.451 --> 00:08:09.188
-You got the beard on
and the chest hair and wigs.

00:08:09.188 --> 00:08:13.024
-Dude, we were turned around,
and you go,

00:08:13.024 --> 00:08:14.159
"Don't mess this up."

00:08:14.159 --> 00:08:15.627
And I go,
"Yeah, yeah, I'm good."

00:08:15.627 --> 00:08:16.728
And you're like,
"No, seriously, bro."

00:08:16.728 --> 00:08:18.163
And I grabbed you
and I was like,

00:08:18.163 --> 00:08:22.168
"No, seriously, this is --
Get this right."

00:08:22.168 --> 00:08:24.036
This is on me. I'm hosting."

00:08:24.036 --> 00:08:25.170
-Oh, I'll never forget it, man.

00:08:25.170 --> 00:08:27.106
And we were just --
I go, "Dude, look at us.

00:08:27.106 --> 00:08:28.941
Look what we're dressed like.
What are you..."

00:08:28.941 --> 00:08:31.110
But it was so serious,
but, like,

00:08:31.110 --> 00:08:33.145
I couldn't look at you,
'cause it was so --

00:08:33.145 --> 00:08:34.279
It was so great.

00:08:34.279 --> 00:08:37.282
I want to talk
about your movie.

00:08:37.282 --> 00:08:38.550
Let's talk about that
when we come back, alright?

00:08:38.550 --> 00:08:39.986
Let's talk about "Palmer."

00:08:39.986 --> 00:08:42.054
More with Justin Timberlake
when we come back, everybody!

